WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.098
Here is a question every new team asks sooner or later.

2
00:00:06.098 --> 00:00:08.631
Your app crashes at three in the morning.

3
00:00:08.631 --> 00:00:09.867
Who starts it again?

4
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.644
Start with what we know.

5
00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:18.140
With docker run, the command that starts one container, you give an order, and Docker carries it out.

6
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:18.461
Once.

7
00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:20.752
An hour later the container dies.

8
00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:24.291
And nothing happens, because Docker already did its job.

9
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.199
docker run is a waiter who serves your dish and then goes home for the night.

10
00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:31.457
Drop your fork, and nobody is coming.

11
00:00:31.457 --> 00:00:36.339
So the point is this: an order is carried out once, and then nobody is watching.

12
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.316
The obvious fix?

13
00:00:38.316 --> 00:00:41.692
Write a little script that checks every minute.

14
00:00:41.692 --> 00:00:44.278
If the app is not running, start it.

15
00:00:44.278 --> 00:00:45.140
Three lines.

16
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:45.499
Done.

17
00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.657
Then a server dies, and the script must move work somewhere else.

18
00:00:49.657 --> 00:00:55.441
Then it must spread copies, add copies when visitors double, and swap versions without a pause.

19
00:00:55.667 --> 00:01:01.292
Soon the little script is a large system of its own, and somebody has to run it and fix it.

20
00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:03.765
Your three lines now need a night shift.

21
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:06.959
So let us say the wish out loud.

22
00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:11.943
Wouldn't it be nice to write down what we want, just once, and have someone keep it true?

23
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.940
Our restaurant has a manager who does exactly that.

24
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.832
She holds a list of what each table should have.

25
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:21.664
Three tables, and each one should have a waiter.

26
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:23.359
She walks the floor.

27
00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:25.746
She counts what is really there.

28
00:01:25.746 --> 00:01:26.939
One, two, three.

29
00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:27.611
All good.

30
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:30.507
Then the waiter at table two calls in sick.

31
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:35.341
On her next pass she reads the list, counts two, and sends for one more.

32
00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:38.284
Nobody told her what to do about sick waiters.

33
00:01:38.284 --> 00:01:39.564
A gap is just a gap.

34
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.070
Let us map it.

35
00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.424
The list is your desired state: what you wrote down.

36
00:01:45.424 --> 00:01:49.294
What she counts is the actual state: what is really running.

37
00:01:49.294 --> 00:01:52.841
And the manager is a small program called a controller.

38
00:01:54.467 --> 00:01:55.658
Now Kubernetes.

39
00:01:55.658 --> 00:02:01.455
You write down one wish: run three copies of hello-devops, version 1.0.0.

40
00:02:01.455 --> 00:02:06.220
Kubernetes stores it as an object, a record in its database.

41
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:10.003
On the other side, the nodes report what is really running.

42
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:14.187
And in between, the controller runs the manager's walk, forever.

43
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.056
Read the desired state.

44
00:02:16.056 --> 00:02:17.856
Observe the actual state.

45
00:02:17.856 --> 00:02:20.880
If the two differ, act to make them match.

46
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:21.744
Then repeat.

47
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:24.684
Now someone deletes one copy by mistake.

48
00:02:24.684 --> 00:02:27.129
Nobody wrote any code for this case.

49
00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:28.216
So what happens?

50
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:35.100
On its next pass, the loop reads three wanted, and counts two running.

51
00:02:35.333 --> 00:02:36.835
So it starts one more.

52
00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:39.770
Just like the manager sending for a waiter.

53
00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.500
A deleted copy is one more gap to close.

54
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:44.490
Now the tempting move.

55
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:49.856
You want one copy gone, so you go straight to the node and stop the container yourself.

56
00:02:49.856 --> 00:02:51.644
You are the boss here, right?

57
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:54.039
Watch the loop.

58
00:02:54.039 --> 00:02:56.417
Three wanted, two running.

59
00:02:56.417 --> 00:02:56.783
Act.

60
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:57.369
Well.

61
00:02:57.369 --> 00:02:58.181
It is back.

62
00:02:58.181 --> 00:03:00.616
Kubernetes is not being stubborn.

63
00:03:00.616 --> 00:03:05.560
It is loyal to the list, not to whoever touched the container last.

64
00:03:05.767 --> 00:03:07.569
So the point really is this.

65
00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:10.467
A change made behind its back does not stick.

66
00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.720
Change the wish, not the container.

67
00:03:12.933 --> 00:03:14.198
Want five copies?

68
00:03:14.198 --> 00:03:16.057
Do not start two by hand.

69
00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:18.735
Edit the wish to five, and apply it.

70
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:23.763
The same loop sees five wanted and three running, and starts two more.

71
00:03:23.763 --> 00:03:24.723
You said what.

72
00:03:24.723 --> 00:03:25.408
Never how.

73
00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:26.945
This loop has a name.

74
00:03:26.945 --> 00:03:31.940
It is called reconciliation, which means bringing two lists back into agreement.

75
00:03:32.167 --> 00:03:34.195
You can see it in every object.

76
00:03:34.195 --> 00:03:36.027
The spec field is your wish.

77
00:03:36.027 --> 00:03:40.281
Kubernetes fills in the status field, its report on what is real.

78
00:03:40.281 --> 00:03:43.095
Controllers work to make status match spec.

79
00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.048
The Kubernetes docs compare it to a thermostat.

80
00:03:46.048 --> 00:03:49.440
You set twenty one degrees, and it keeps reading the room.

81
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.896
You never tell it to heat for ten minutes.

82
00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.175
Most kinds of object have a controller, sometimes several.

83
00:03:56.175 --> 00:03:58.871
So here is a habit for the rest of this part.

84
00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:02.107
When you meet a new kind of object, ask two questions.

85
00:04:02.333 --> 00:04:03.892
What does its spec ask for?

86
00:04:03.892 --> 00:04:06.144
And which controller makes that happen?

87
00:04:06.867 --> 00:04:13.207
And notice the difference from Terraform and Ansible, which compare code with reality only while they run.

88
00:04:13.207 --> 00:04:16.736
This loop checks all the time, with nobody at the keyboard.

89
00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:19.563
So, the moral of the story.

90
00:04:19.563 --> 00:04:22.490
An order runs once, and then nobody watches.

91
00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:25.949
A wish is kept true, by a loop that never goes home.

92
00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:27.545
You write what you want.

93
00:04:27.545 --> 00:04:29.341
The loop works out the how.

94
00:04:29.567 --> 00:04:31.359
One quick check before you go.

95
00:04:31.359 --> 00:04:34.706
The wish says three, and the nodes report three running.

96
00:04:34.706 --> 00:04:36.797
What does the loop do on this pass?

97
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.450
Nothing at all.

98
00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.428
There is no gap, so there is nothing to act on.

99
00:04:43.428 --> 00:04:45.899
It simply looks again on the next pass.
